{"id":"palindromic-substrings","title":"Palindromic Substrings","difficulty":"Medium","pattern":"1-D Dynamic Programming","tags":["String","Dynamic Programming","Two Pointers"],"companies":{"google":2,"meta":3,"amazon":2,"microsoft":2},"description":"Given a string `s`, count how many of its substrings are palindromes. Substrings at different positions count separately even if their text is identical.","examples":[{"input":"s = \"abc\"","output":"3","explanation":"Only the three single characters."},{"input":"s = \"aaa\"","output":"6","explanation":"\"a\" three times, \"aa\" twice, \"aaa\" once."}],"constraints":["1 <= s.length <= 1000","s consists of lowercase English letters."],"hints":["Each palindrome has a unique centre: a character (odd length) or a gap between two characters (even length).","Expand from every centre while the two ends match; each successful step is one more palindrome."],"function":"countSubstrings","signature":"def countSubstrings(self, s: str) -> int:","arg_types":{"s":"str"},"return_type":"int","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"s":"abc"},"output":3},{"input":{"s":"aaa"},"output":6},{"input":{"s":"a"},"output":1},{"input":{"s":"abba"},"output":6},{"input":{"s":""},"output":45150},{"input":{"s":""},"output":4998}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def countSubstrings(self, s: str) -> int:\n        n = len(s)\n        count = 0\n        for centre in range(n):\n            for lo, hi in ((centre, centre), (centre, centre + 1)):\n                while lo >= 0 and hi < n and s[lo] == s[hi]:\n                    count += 1\n                    lo -= 1\n                    hi += 1\n        return count\n","approach":"**Group palindromes by their centre.** For each of the `2n - 1` centres (n characters, n-1 gaps), expand outward while the end characters match; every successful expansion is exactly one palindrome, and every palindrome is produced exactly once by its own centre. Summing gives the count.\n\n**Why it works:** if `s[lo..hi]` is a palindrome centred at c, so is every shorter substring with the same centre, so the sequence of matches from the centre outward is a prefix of the expansion; the first mismatch ends all longer candidates at that centre.\n\n**DP alternative:** `pal[i][j] = (s[i] == s[j]) and (j - i < 2 or pal[i+1][j-1])`, counting the true cells. Same O(n^2) time but O(n^2) space; worth describing because it generalises to related problems, but present the expansion.\n\n**Common mistakes:** only handling odd centres (\"abba\" gives 4 instead of 6), counting substrings by text rather than by position, and checking every substring with a reversal (O(n^3)).\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Return the longest one instead (track the best expansion). Count distinct palindromic substrings by text (collect them in a set, or use a palindromic tree / Eertree). Can you do O(n)? (Manacher gives the radius at every centre; sum of (radius+1)/2 terms.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n^2) worst case","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["longest-palindromic-substring","palindrome-partitioning"],"number":130,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def countSubstrings(self, s: str) -> int:\n        pass\n"}